## 1. Introduction
Since the development of highly efficient third-generation photovoltaic devices, perovskite solar cells (PSCs) has witnessed an outstanding success, its power conversion efficiency (PCE) has exceeded $25\%$ from $3.8\%$ over the past decade.[1] A typical PSC is composed of five parts: transparent conductive substrate (TCS), electron-transporting layer (ETL), perovskite light absorption layer, hole-transporting layer (HTL), and metal contact electrode.[2,3] Generally, PSCs require a mesoporous semiconductor oxide layer to support perovskite light absorption layer and also act as an ETL to facilitate the injection of the carriers into the TCS.[4].
Mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ can meet both functions and is the most common mesoporous materials. However, common $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ suffers from poor conductivity and a low carrier diffusion rate.[5] Great efforts have been made to improve the performance of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs. For instance, doping a low concentration of metals $\cdot\mathsf{L}\mathsf{a}^{2+}$ [6], $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ [7], $\mathsf{N b}^{5+}$ [8], $\mathrm{Er}^{3+}$ [9], ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ [10], et al) into a $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETM can improve its optoelectronic properties, which in turn improves the photovoltaic performance of the PSCs. The interfacial modification of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETM is another strategy to elevate the carriers transport capability of the ETL and improve the interfacial conditions of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL and perovskite light-absorbing layer. To date, graphene and its derivatives[11–13], ionic liquid[14] and metal–organic frameworks (MOFs)[15] have been employed to accelerate electron transport and improve the crystallinity of perovskite films, which play a critical role in high-performance PSCs[16–18]. Our group applied thioacetamide as an interlayer to anchor the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL which was aimed to passivate the traps at the interface between $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL and perovskite layer. The trap density was reduced from $3.36\times10^{16}\mathrm{~\bar{c}m}^{-3}$ to $1.9\dot{3}\times10^{16}\mathrm{~cm}^{-3}$ and the highest PCE of $19.14\%$ was achieved for PSC.[19] Hou and co-authors used cesium acetate (CsAC) to modify the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL which resulted in an alleviation of non-radiative losses in the PSC. The PCE of the CsACmodified PSC was improved from $18.6\%$ (control PSC) to $20.1\%$ [20] Furthermore, the preparation of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs with different architectures is also an effective approach to boost PSC photovoltaic performance. A variety of ordered $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ structures such as nanotubes[21], nanorods[22] and nanowires[23] have been utilized as ETLs for PSCs. $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs with highly ordered architectures promote carrier transportation and the collection of charges, which can inhibit the rate of electron-hole recombination at interfaces. Among them, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesocrystals with an ordered hierarchical superstructure have received great interest for use in solar cells. [24–27] Ascribe to its particle of mesocrystalline material that is composed of many ultra-small submits along specific crystallographic orientations[28], thus, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesocrystals possess many internal porous structure and a large specific surface area. Therefore, our group first applied a mesoporous film composed of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesocrystals as the ETL for PSC, which resulted in a high PCE of $20.26\%$ [25]. Such a PCE based on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesocrystal ETL based PSCs was due to improved electron extraction and the inhibition of charge accumulation at interfaces between the perovskite layer and ETL. Robertson and co-workers immobilized $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesocrystals with (0 0 1) exposed facets for dye-sensitized solar cells (DSSCs) [24]. Thanks to the features of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesocrystals with high surface area and high active exposed facets, the photo-anodes of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesocrystals possess superior dye pick-up capability and optimal electron-transporting ability which resulted in an outstanding fill factor for DSSCs.
In this paper, cube-like anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesocrystals have been successfully synthesized via employing amorphous titanate as the precursor. The obtained $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesocrystals displayed a uniform cube-like shape with an average size of ca. $100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . Meanwhile, it is interesting that the mesopore size centered at $6\mathrm{nm}$ and macropores at $100\mathrm{nm}$ for the anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesocrystals were believed to benefit the immersion of the perovskite precursor solution and therefore improve the efficiency of PSCs. An average PCE of $18.54\%$ for the anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesocrystals ETL based PSCs was achieved. The optimized PSC device acquires a champion PCE of $20.05\%$ , accompanied by superior stability to a commercial $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL based PSC.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Synthesis of amorphous titanate precursor
Typically, $30~\mathrm{g}$ of NaOH (Aladdin, $99.9\%$ was added into $70~\mathrm{mL}$ mixed solvent solution, which contained $35~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water and $35~\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol absolute (Aladdin, AR). Then, $1.5{\mathrm{g}}$ of anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles (Macklin, $99.8\%$ $30~\mathrm{{nm}}$ ) was dispersed into it and kept stirring constantly with $^\textrm{\scriptsize2h}$ under room temperture. Finally, the suspension was transferred into a Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave with a capacity of $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ and kept at $180^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . The product was washed with 0.10 M HCl solution for several times until the pH was less than 7 and dried at $70^{\circ}\mathsf C$ in air overnight. The final sample will be used as a precursor for fabricating anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesocrystals.
## 2.2. Synthesis of anatase $T_{1}{O_{2}}$ mesocrystals
Typically, $300~\mathrm{mg}$ of precursor was dispersed into $70~\mathrm{mL}$ of 1.5 M $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ solution and then $3\mathrm{mM}$ of dodecylbenzenesulphonic acid (ADBS) was added dropwise into it. After stirring for $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ , the white emulsion was then kept stirring at $70~^{\circ}C$ for 5 days. The final sample was washed extensively with ethanol and deionized water for several times and then dried at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in air overnight. For removing the residual organics, the as prepared sample was calcined at $550^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ (donated as Meso-TiO2).
## 2.3. Preparation of substrates and electron-transporting layer
The F-doped $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ coated glasses (FTO) was used as a substrate and etched by Zn powder and 2 M HCl firstly. Then, the etched FTO substrates were cleaned successively with detergent, deionized water, ethanol, acetone and isopropanol each for $30\mathrm{min}$ in a ultrasonic cleaner, respectively. The FTO substrates were dried at $70^{\circ}\mathsf C$ in clean air and then treated with a UV-ozone machine for $15\mathrm{min}$ for removing any organic residues and to make then more hydrophilic before use.
For preparing electron transport layer, the FTO substrates were coated with a compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer by spin-coating and using $0.15\mathrm{M}$ of titanium diisopropoxide bis(acetylacetonate) $(75~\mathrm{wt\%}$ in isopropanol, Alfa-Aesar) in 1-butanol $(99.9\%$ , Alfa-Aesar) as a precursor solution. After spin-coating, the FTO substrates were annealed at $550^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ and then natural cooled down to room temperature. Subsequently, the mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer was deposited by spin-coating for $30~s$ at $2000\ \mathrm{rpm\min^{-1}}$ , using cube-like anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesocrystals and commercial anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles $(25\mathrm{\textrm{~}}\mathrm{nm}$ , donated as ${\mathsf{C o m}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}.$ ) paste diluted in ethanol $(0.1\mathrm{~g~mL}^{-1},$ . Finally, the substrates were immediately dried at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ and annealed again at $550^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ .
## 2.4. Preparation of the perovskite layer
The perovskite precursor solution containing (1 M), $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ $(1.1\ \mathrm{M})$ , MABr $(0.2~\mathrm{M})$ and $\mathsf{P b B r}_{2}$ ${\bf\dot{\langle}0.2\textbf{M\rangle}}$ in a mixed solution of anhydrous DMF: DMSO $(\boldsymbol{\mathsf{v}};\boldsymbol{\mathsf{v}}\ =\ 4;1$ ) was spin-coated on the electron-transporting layer in a program at 1000 and $6000\ \mathrm{rpm\min^{-1}}$ for $10~s$ and $20~\mathsf{s}$ , respectively. And the $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of chlorobenzene was dropped onto the spinning substrate for $5~s$ prior to the end of the program. Then, the substrate was annealed at $100~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ . All the steps mentioned above were conducted in the nitrogen-filled glovebox.
## 2.5. Preparation of the hole-transporting layer and Au layer
The hole-transporting layer was spin-coated with a spiroOMeTAD solution at $4000\ \mathrm{rpm\min^{-1}}$ for $20~\mathsf{s}.$ where $1~\mathrm{mL}$ solution of spiroOMeTAD in chlorobenzene $(72.3\ \mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}}$ ) was dissolved with the addition of $28.8~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ 4-tert-butylpyridine and $17.5~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of a stock solution of $520~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}$ lithium bis(trifluoromethylsulpho nyl)imide in acetonitrile. Ultimately, an Au layer was thermally evaporated on the hole-transporting layer as the back electrode. The active area was $0.06~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ .
## 2.6. Characterizations of the samples
X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of the samples were recorded on an X-ray diffraction with a copper target (Rigaku SmartLab). The morphologies of the samples were observed via the scanning electron microscopy (FESEM, Hitachi S4800) and the transmission electron microscopy (TEM, JEOL JEM2010 instrument). $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption analysis was carried out on a Micro-meritics ASAP 2020 instrument.
## 2.7. Measurements of the photovoltaic properties for perovskite solar cells
The steady-state photoluminescence (PL) and time-resolved photoluminescence spectroscopy (TR-PL) of the perovskite film deposited on Meso- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL and Com- $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL were performed using Edinburgh FLS 980 instrument with the excitation at $406\mathrm{nm}$ . The photovoltaic performance of the PSCs was recorded through utilizing a source meter (Keithley 2400). A PEC-L11 AM 1.5 solar simulator with a 1000 W Xe lamp and an AM 1.5 filter (Peccell) was employed as the light source $(100\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}).$ . The incident photo-to-photo conversion efficiency (IPCE) spectra were collected using an instrument of PEC-S20 (Peccell). Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was recorded via an electrochemical workstation (IM6, Zahner) and the data was fitted by using Zview software.
## 3. Results and discussion
The anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesocrystalline material was synthesized by using amorphous titanate as a precursor (Figure S1) and a tentative mechanism for the formation of cube-like $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesocrystal was illustrated in Scheme S1. The morphology of the anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesocrystal is shown in Fig. 1. The low magnification SEM image (Fig. 1a) indicates that the as-prepared sample had a wellregulated shape and uniform size of ca. $100\mathrm{nm}$ , which was further confirmed by the low magnification TEM image (Fig. 1a). The particles were composed of many ultra-small subunits that can be clearly confirmed by the high magnification SEM image (inset in
Fig. 1a), resulting in a very rough surface. This was further verified by the high magnification TEM image, as shown in Fig. 1c. The selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern (Fig. 1d) taken from a single nanoparticle displayed a typical ‘‘single-crystal-like” diffraction pattern with a slightly elongated spot. This phenomenon has been observed in many mesocrystalline materials, which resulted from the lattice mismatch of the boundaries between the ultra-tiny subunits[29]. The different diffraction spots in the diffraction pattern corresponded to the (100), (010), and (220) lattice planes of anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , indicating the exposed (001) facet of the anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles, as depicted in Fig. 1f. The HRTEM image (Fig. 1e) reveals that the ultra-small subunits were about $5\mathrm{nm}$ .
Fresh cube-like anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesocrystals particles were furtherly calcined at $550^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for 30 min. The morphologies of the calcined $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesocrystals were characterized by SEM and TEM, as depicted in Fig. 2. The low magnification SEM image (Fig. 2a) reveals that the morphologies and sizes of the particles were mostly unchanged. However, the surfaces became rougher compared with the fresh sample (inset in Fig. 1a), resulting in a rich porous structure with enlarged pores. This result was further confirmed by TEM analysis (Fig. 2c and d). The SEAD pattern in Fig. 2e also shows a typical ‘‘single-crystal-like” diffraction spots of mesocrystalline materials, which resulted from the aggregation of ultra-small submits along the same crystallographic orientation. [30,31].
The $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms and pore size distribution are displayed in Figure S2. The specific surface area of the asobtained $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesocrystal $(M e s o{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2})$ ) based on the BrunauerEmmett-Teller (BET) method was determined to be $51.3~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Moreover, the pore size distribution calculated by the nonlocal density functional theory (NLDFT) method demonstrates that the most of the pores inside $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesocrystal nanoparticles were mainly mesopores $(6~\mathrm{{nm})}$ and macropores $\cdot100\ \mathrm{nm}\rangle$ , respectively. Such a suitable pore size inside the nanoparticles will benefit the immersion of the perovskite precursor solution, resulting in a higher perovskite loading.[32].
Fig. 3a and 3b illustrate the plan-view SEM images of $M e s o-\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and Com- $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ based ETLs, respectively. It can be observed that $M e s o-\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nano-crystals were overlaid on the compact layer to form a thin $M e s o{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesoporous film. The $M e s o{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesoporous film was much smoother than the Com- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ based film. As well known, flat mesoporous ETLs possess a superior contact with perovskite film, thus, retarding carrier recombination at the interface and leading to the enhanced photovoltaic performance of the PSCs.[16] Fig. 3c, d depict the surface morphologies of the perovskite layers deposited on $M e s o{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and ${\mathsf{C o m}}{\mathsf{-T i O}}_{2}$ ETLs, respectively. As seen in the respective SEM images, the grains of the perovskite film based on $M e s o{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ exhibited slightly larger and more well-regulated compared with those of the perovskite film based on ${\mathsf{C o m}}{\mathsf{-T i O}}_{2}$ . Furthermore, as revealed in Figure S3, the average grain size of perovskite deposited on $M e s o-\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL was $48.34~\mathrm{nm}$ which was slightly larger than that of $46.87~\mathrm{nm}$ for the crystallite grain size peroskite deposited on ${\mathsf{C o m}}{\mathsf{-T i O}}_{2}$ ETL. The Scherrer equation was employed as (1):
$$
W=\frac{k\gamma}{D\cos\theta}
$$
where $W$ is the average crystallite size, $K$ is a shape factor, D is the full width at half maximum of the reflection, and $\theta$ is the angular position. On the basis of the characteristic diffraction peak at $14^{\circ}$ for perovskite crystal, the value of $D$ for the Meso$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL based perovskite was $0.244^{\circ}$ , thus $W_{\mathrm{Meso-TiO2}}$ was $32.46~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . Moreover, the value of D for the Com- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL based perovskite was $0.258^{\circ}$ , thereby the $W_{\mathsf{C o m}}$ -TiO2 was $30.69\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The results calculated by the Sherrer equation demonstrate that the particle size of the $M e s o{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL based perovskite crystal was slightly larger than that of the Com- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL based perovskite crystal.
As shown in Fig. S4a, the diffraction peaks in the XRD patterns of the $(\mathrm{FAPbI_{3}})_{1-\mathrm{x}}(\mathrm{MAPbBr_{3}})_{\mathrm{x}}$ based perovskite films matched the typical peaks of the perovskite black phase (photoactive $\mathsf{\Omega}\propto$ - phase). The optical absorption spectra of different perovskite films shown in Fig. S2b also reveal that the perovskite film based on $M e s o-\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL provided a higher absorbance in contrast to that of the perovskite film based on COM- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL. Furthermore, Meso$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL based PSCs were fabricated with the structure of FTO/- compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer/mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer/perovskite layer/spiroOMeTAD/Au (Fig. 4e-f). It can be seen from Fig. 4e, the layers were in close contact with each other, indicating that the preferred interfacial condition dominated the improvement in the PCE and reduction in the hysteresis index for PSCs. Fig. 4e shows that each individual layer and interface was distinct. However, the Meso- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesoporous layer was unclear indicating that the bottom of the perovskite film has been successfully penetrated and filled into the pores among the $M e s o{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ film.
To explore the photovoltaic performances of the different ETLs based PSCs, the results are revealed in Fig. 4a. The relevant $J{-}V$ parameters are listed in Table 1. The Com- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL based device achieved a PCE of $18.25\%$ with a $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ (short-circuit current density) of $24.19\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ (open-circuit voltage) of $1.03\mathrm{~V~}$ and FF (fill factor) of 0.73. In contrast, the device based on $M e s o{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL exhibited $J_{s c}$ of $24.58\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $1.04\mathrm{V}$ and FF of 0.76, yielding a PCE of $19.43\%$ with a champion value of $20.05\%$ (Figure S5). The superior photovoltaic performance of the $M e s o-\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL based PSC was attributed to its higher $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and FF which were caused by facilitated charge carrier transfer and the decreased defect density.[33] To assess the accuracy and reproducibility of the photovoltaic performance improvements for $M e s o-\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL based PSCs, 30 cells from each group were fabricated and the PCE statistics for the control and $M e s o-\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL based PSCs were presented in Fig. 4b. It notes that $M e s o-\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL based PSCs had a better average PCE of $18.54\%$ which was $0.64\%$ higher than that of ${\mathsf{C o m}}{\mathsf{-T i O}}_{2}$ ETL based PSCs $(17.89\%)$ . To test the stability of the PSCs, cells $(\mathrm{PCE}~>~18\%)$ for each group were kept in a dry-box $(25\ \ ^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ $25\pm5\%$ RH) for 20 days and their PCE was tested every $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The results were collected and shown in Fig. 4d, the device based on $M e s o-\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL can maintain $80\%$ of its original PCE after 20 days in contrast to $58\%$ for the control device. The improved stability for $M e s o{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL based PSCs was probably attributed to the fact that the high quality of the electron-transporting layer could effectively reduce trap densities and suppress the charge carrier recombination at interface within the perovskite film and ETL.[34].
Fig. 4c demonstrates the incident photon-to-electron conversion efficiency (IPCE) spectra for PSCs composed of $M e s o-\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL and ${\mathsf{C o m}}{\mathsf{-T i O}}_{2}$ ETL, respectively. The cell based on $M e s o{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL presented a higher value in the visible spectrum. The value of $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ calculated by integrating the IPCE curve was $23.36~\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ which was consistent with the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ value derived from the $J$ -V curve. Aiming to reveal the reason for the improved $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of the Meso- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL based PSC, as shown in Fig. 5a, the results imply that the cube-like $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesocrystal were aggregated orderly which could supply a smooth and flat film. Moreover, the high number of pores within the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesocrystal’s internal structure favored the infiltration of the perovskite precursor solution. This in turn could help load more perovskite absorber and improve the contact between the perovskite layer and ETL. Nevertheless, as shown in Fig. 5b, the commercial $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL was rough and randomly distributed which may hinder the electron transport and increase the rate of the carrier recombination.[7].
To further investigate the effect of the $M e s o{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL on the reduction of the trap states, the carrier recombination process of the perovskite film was analyzed by steady-state photoluminescence (PL) and time-resolved photoluminescence (TR-PL) spectroscopy. The PL spectra of the perovskite film deposited on $M e s o-\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL and Com- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL are presented in Fig. 6a. The perovskite film based on $M e s o{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL exhibited a decreased PL intensity compared with that of the perovskite film deposited on ${\mathsf{C o m}}{\mathsf{-T i O}}_{2}$ ETL. It has been reported that a reduced intensity of the PL spectra implies the suppression of non-radiative charge recombination between the interface of the light-absorbing layer and ETL.[35,36] In addition, the TR-PL decay lifetime also decreased for the Meso- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL based perovskite film, as shown in Fig. 6b. The corresponding results were fitted using a biexponential decay equation (2):
$$
y=A_{1}e^{-x/T_{2}}A_{2}e^{-x/T_{2}}+y o
$$
The parameters are listed in Table S1. These results reveal that the lifetime of the Meso- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2},$ /perovskite sample was significantly shorter than that of the Com- $.\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/$ perovskite sample. Hence, the transfer of photo-induced electrons from the perovskite to Meso$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL occurred more quickly than that from perovskite to Com- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL. The improved charge extraction and transport ability decreased the charge accumulation at the interface, leading to an enhanced $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ value and elevated photovoltaic performance for the Meso-TiO2 ETL based PSCs.[37] The series resistance $(\mathsf{R}_{s})$ and recombination resistance $\left(\mathbb{R}_{\mathrm{rec}}\right)$ of different ETLs based PSCs were determined via electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS). The corresponding Nyquist plots are shown in Fig. 6c and the related data are given in Table S2. In contrast to the commercial $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL, the cell based on the Meso- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL had a lower $\mathtt{R}_{s}$ (9.4 vs $10.2\ \Omega\ '$ and a larger $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{R}}_{\mathrm{rec}}$ ( $1940\ \mathrm{vs}\ 1666\ \Omega$ . The results validate that a lower $\mathtt{R}_{s}$ and a higher $\mathrm{\sfR}_{\mathrm{rec}}$ for the $M e s o{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL based PSC, indicating less current loss and a lower rate of electron-hole recombination. The $J{-}V$ curves of the device based on $M e s o-\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL obtained under different sweep directions are shown in Fig. S6a. The results indicate a difference in the value of the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ between reverse sweep and forward sweep stemming from the appearance of hysteretic phenomenon. The hysteresis of the device was ascribed to the unbalanced flux between electrons and holes from the perovskite layer into the ETL and HTL, separately.[38] To ensure the accuracy of the $J{-}V$ results, the current density of the $M e s o-\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL based cell was performed with $0.88{\mathrm{~V~}}$ (voltage from the maximum power output point) under illumination, as displayed in Fig. S6b. A stabilized efficiency of $18.31\%$ was 461010485330500obtained for at least $100s.$ The diversity between the stable efficiency and PCE of the PSC was possibly due to the hysteresis of the device as demonstrated above.
## 4. Conclusion
Herein, cube-like anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesocrystals were facilely synthesized via a hydrothermal method and utilized as an ETM for PSCs. The champion devices based on Meso- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL exhibited a satisfying PCE of exceeding $20\%$ and $80\%$ of the initial PCE was maintained after being stored in ambient condition for 20 days at $25\pm5\%$ relative humidity. This outstanding photovoltaic performance of the Meso$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL based PSCs was attributed to the intrinsic properties of the cube-like $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesocrystals. Due to its highly ordered subunits and suitable pore sizes, the $M e s o-\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL could support the intrusion of more perovskite absorber than the ${\mathsf{C o m}}{\mathsf{-T i O}}_{2}$ ETL. This resulted in more efficient charge extraction from the lightabsorbing layer to the Meso- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL and suppressed electron-hole recombination at the interface between perovskite and Meso- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL. Hence, cube-like anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ can be employed in future research as a promising candidate for optoelectronic devices.
